#~
Load-time verifier false refusal: the and/or slot reused for a closure and a literal.

The compiler keeps one scratch slot (the "and/or" slot, local 0) and reuses it
for unrelated temporaries. Here it holds a lambda's closure inside an `if` in a
do-while loop, then the constant-folded selector of a `select` (the literal -9).
At the loop's join the verifier met the slot's two values although nothing
reads the slot there (it is rewritten before every use), so the literal looked
like the closure that is read through as an object, and the load was refused:
"Invalid bytecode: ... an integer literal used as a reference" (-opt s3 only).
The nightly differential fuzzer found it in about 10% of its programs.
~#

class VerifierAndOrSlotClosure {
  function : F0(p0 : Int) ~ Int {
    w0 := 0;
    do {
      if(w0 < 3) {
        k0 := FuncRef->New(\() ~ IntRef : () => IntRef->New(p0))<IntRef>;
      };
      select(((0 - 9) % 41)) {
        label -13: {
        }
        label 19: {
        }
      };
      w0 += 1;
    } while(w0 < 8);
    return p0 + w0;
  }

  function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
    r := F0(5);
    if(r <> 13) {
      "FAIL"->PrintLine();
      Runtime->Exit(1);
    };
    "PASS"->PrintLine();
  }
}
